ulation of one, so behaviour, error messages and types are what you would get from ${t} anywhere else. The database is yours for the session and can be reset from the header.`]}),e.showCompilerFaq){const o=[`Both. The page is an online ${t} editor and it compiles and runs what you write.`];e.isJava&&(o.push(" Nothing is simulated: your code is built with ",{code:"javac"}),o.push(" and executed on a real JDK.")),n.push({question:`Is this an online ${t} editor or a compiler?`,answer:o})}if(e.canDebug){const o=[`Yes. JDoodle has a step debugger for ${t}. Click a line number to set a breakpoint, press Debug, and the program stops there so you can step over, step into, and read the call stack.`];o.push(e.debugShowsVariables?" Local variables are listed with their values while you are paused, and you can open objects and arrays to look inside.":` Breakpoints and stepping work for ${t}, though locals are not listed while paused because this language has no variable inspector.`),e.debugVersionPhrase&&o.push(` Debugging runs on ${e.debugVersionPhrase}, the newest build here and the one this page opens with.`),o.push(" Debugging is free, and a session runs for three minutes on the free plan or ten on a paid one."),n.push({question:`Can I debug ${t} code online?`,answer:o})}if(e.canShowGui){const o=[`Yes. A ${t} program that opens a window shows it here in the GUI tab, live, next to your Output. Tick GUI in the I/O row before you run, and the window appears as soon as your program draws one - you can click and type into it as you would on your own machine.`];e.guiVersionLabels.length&&o.push(` The GUI window runs on ${e.guiVersionLabels.join(", ")}, the newest build here and the one this page opens with.`),o.push(" A window stays open for five minutes on the free plan or fifteen on a paid one, and every click or keystroke extends it."),n.push({question:`Can I see a ${t} GUI window online?`,answer:o})}return e.needsGdbExplainer&&n.push({question:`Is this an online ${t} GDB?`,an